  slide "Cache-pressure warmup" {
    eyebrow: "Cache-pressure warmup"
    title: "Extended warmup redispatches the boundary credit under pressure"
    lede: "Cache-pressure warmup is the extended phase: lanes redispatch a lane-salted boundary-priming credit (`rewrite_for_warmup`) against the server, cycling through the corpus, so KV stays under load before the handoff drain — not the post-`t*` live set."

    narration: "Duration-bounded lanes repeatedly draw the next corpus template, apply the boundary rewrite at that lane's salted t*, and dispatch the resulting flattened, one-node-per-chain priming credit — a fresh instance id each time, no idle delay. The goal is cache pressure: keep the inference server's KV busy with priming traffic until the clock deadline, then drain into the handoff. The real post-t* remainder is not replayed here; profiling's frontier chop resumes it later."

    term: {
      word: "cache-pressure warmup"
      meaning: "Extended warmup that redispatches a lane-salted boundary-priming credit (`rewrite_for_warmup`) on pressure lanes for a duration budget, building KV load before profiling handoff."
    }

    points: [
      "`GraphPressureRecycle` runs until Clock deadline; lanes share one corpus cursor.",
      "Each dispatch draws the next corpus template and applies `rewrite_for_warmup`'s flattened boundary credit — one node per live chain, rooted at START with no delay — NOT the post-`t*` live set.",
      "Distinct from ordinary phase warmup (same rewrite, single pass) and from the later profiling resume (`chop_trie_at_frontier`).",
      "Evidence: `rust/runtime/src/graph/snapshot.rs` — `rewrite_for_warmup`; `graph_phase_runtime.rs` — `PressureTemplate` / `GraphPressureRecycle`."
    ]

    caption: "Boundary credit · recycle under pressure · then drain."

